-- EVERY-EACH-QUANTIFIER-SPEC §6.1.1, R-T3 (adversarial pass round 2,
-- R2-TC-1): a LIST after `BREACH BY` names several parties, one per element,
-- unless the rule's party type is itself that list type, in which case it
-- names ONE party whose value is a list. Which reading is meant is decided by
-- the party type — so a BREACH that is reached by INFERENCE, with nothing
-- upstream fixing its party type, cannot decide it and is refused HERE, at
-- the BREACH, naming the two ways out. Guessing the element reading instead
-- pinned the party type to the element type and failed later, at the use
-- site, naming the wrong place — and passed with the RAND's operands swapped.
--
-- Both shapes that arrive with a fresh party type are here. Each is fixed by
-- a GIVETH on the definition (ok/every/run-blame.l4, `the pair delivers` and
-- `the pair, breach first`), or, for the RAND, by writing the PARTY operand
-- first.

DECLARE Step IS ONE OF deliver

-- 1. A top-level MEANS with no GIVETH: nothing says the party type.
`the pair fails` MEANS
    BREACH BY (LIST "a", "b") BECAUSE "pair"

-- 2. The LEFT operand of a RAND with no GIVETH: the compound is inferred with
--    a fresh party type, and the left operand is checked before the right
--    (the PARTY) has fixed it.
`pair` MEANS
    (BREACH BY (LIST "a", "b") BECAUSE "pair")
    RAND
    (PARTY (LIST "a", "b") MUST deliver WITHIN 5)
